hat. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard B3 or B4, I lose that possibility. \n\nSo maybe it's better to discard a tile that's not part of a potential chow. \n\nLooking at my hand, T2 is a single tile not part of any chow. Maybe discard T2. \n\nThe unshown count for T2 is 3. If I play T2, someone might need it for a chow. But since I have a Chi of T6-8, maybe T2 is isolated. \n\nYes, T2 is not part of any existing or potential chow. So playing T2 is safe. \n\nSo the valid action includes Play T2. \n\nIf I play T2, it's a safe discard. \n\nSo maybe that's the best move. \n\nBut why is T2 a valid action? The valid actions are [Play T9; Play B3; Play T2; Play B5; Play F4; Play B9; Play B8; Play W8; Play F2; Play W9; Play B4]. \n\nYes, Play T2 is an option. \n\nBy playing T2, I remove a tile that's not part of any chow, and it's a middle tile in the Bamboo suit. But since I have a Chi of T6-8, maybe T2 is not useful. \n\nSo, the best move is to play T2. \n\nThis would allow me to keep the B3, B4, B5 for a potential chow, and the other tiles. \n\nTherefore, the answer is Play T2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding T2 is the safest option as it is not part of any potential chow or pung and has a moderate unshown count. This preserves the possibility of forming chows in the Dots suit (B3-B5) and avoids losing a seat wind (F2) or risking a last tile (B5).]\nReason: [T2 is isolated and not part of any existing or potential melds, making it a low-risk discard. Keeping B3, B4, B5 intact supports forming a chow in the Dots suit, while preserving F2 (seat wind) and B5 (last tile) for scoring opportunities.]\nAnswer: Play T2"}
